Sand seems a bit of an odd idea to me.  Nonetheless, while we're
discussing it, here's my two cents' worth:

Hallucinating players should see sandcastles, beach umbrellas and deck
chairs.

Hallucinating tourists should get a message saying "Finally - now
when's the tide coming?" or some silly message in that area.

In a similar vein, #sitting on a sand tile with a fountain or a water
tile in an adjacent square should make hp regenerate faster.  If
hallucinating, you should get a message saying "you get a nice suntan".

Items on sand tiles should be 'invisible' - the player would have to
walk over every tile and feel for items on it as though he were blind.
Normal item detection spells would work as normal.

Drinking uncursed or holy water while on a sand tile, or quaffing from
a fountain while next to a sand tile regains a few hp, and gives the
message "you quench your thirst".

Perhaps wearing heavy armour, particularily metal armour should damage
you or cause your speed to drop.

If you (T)ake off some item of footwear while on a sand tile, you
should get a message "your <foo>s are full of sand".  This probably
won't work, but maybe walking barefoot over the scorching sands would
damage the charcter, but never kill him?

Sand shouldn't be seen too often.  Maybe it's a room type, or maybe
there will be a chance that a level is all sand.

Or perhaps there should be an extra branch featuring a sand-covered
bonus level.  This could be dune-themed, pirate-themed, or maybe
Arabian nights themed.

Perhaps a level in the archeologist's quest level should be partially
covered in sand.

I hope you like a couple of the ideas.



